4. Destructuring 
Destructuring allows values to be extracted from arrays or objects into 
variables. 
let numbers = [10, 20]; 
let [a, b] = numbers; 

let student = { name: "ABC", age: 20 }; 
let { name, age } = student; 

console.log(a, b); 
console.log(name, age); 
Output: 
10 20 
ABC 20 
 Array destructuring uses []. 
 Object destructuring uses {}. 
 Makes extracting values shorter and cleaner. 
Remember: Destructuring → Extract values from arrays/objects easily  
5. Default Parameters 
Default parameters provide a default value when a function argument is 
not supplied. 
function greet(name = "Guest") { 
    console.log(`Hello ${name}`); 
} 

greet(); 
greet("ABC");

Output: 
Hello Guest 
Hello ABC 
 The default value is used when the argument is missing or undefined. 
 Makes functions easier and safer to use. 
Remember: Default Parameter → Default value for a missing argument  
6. Rest and Spread Operator 
The ... syntax is used as Rest to collect values and Spread to expand values.  
Rest Operator 
function sum(...numbers) { 
    return numbers[0] + numbers[1] + numbers[2];  
} 

console.log(sum(10, 20, 30)); 
Output: 60 
Spread Operator 
let nums = [10, 20, 30]; 
let newNums = [...nums, 40]; 

console.log(newNums); 
Output: [10, 20, 30, 40] 
 Rest → collects multiple values into one variable. 
 Spread → expands elements of an array or object.  
 Both use the ... syntax. 
Remember: Rest → Collect | Spread → Expand

7. Classes 
A class provides a blueprint for creating objects. ES6 introduced class syntax 
to make object-oriented programming more convenient.  
class Student { 
    constructor(name, age) { 
        this.name = name; 
        this.age = age; 
    } 

    display() { 
        console.log(this.name, this.age); 
    } 
} 

let student = new Student("ABC", 20); 
student.display(); 
Output: ABC 20 
 class defines the class. 
 constructor() initializes object data. 
 this refers to the current object. 
 new creates an object from the class. 
8. Modules 
Modules allow JavaScript code to be divided into separate files and shared 
between them using export and import. 
Export 
export const name = "ABC";

Import 
import { name } from "./student.js"; 

console.log(name); 
 export makes code available to other modules.  
 import uses code exported from another module.  
 Modules help organize large applications into separate files.  
9. for...of 
The for...of loop is used to iterate over the values of an iterable such as an 
array. 
let numbers = [10, 20, 30]; 

for (let number of numbers) { 
    console.log(number); 
} 
Output: 
10    20    30 
 for...of gives values from the iterable. 
 It is commonly used with arrays and other iterable collections.  
10. Map 
Map is a collection that stores data as key-value pairs. 
let student = new Map(); 

student.set("name", "ABC"); 
student.set("age", 20); 

console.log(student.get("name"));
